---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";
import { iphoneAppUrl } from "../../data/downloads";
---

<DocsLayout slug="phone">
  <p>
    OpenOrc for iPhone connects straight to OpenOrc on your computer, with no server in between. The agents keep running on the computer, and everything the phone shows comes from it. If you set up a cloud brain, the phone can also reach
    it on your own cloud account, while your computer sleeps.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="pair">
    <h2 id="pair">Pair your phone</h2>
    <p>Install OpenOrc on your iPhone from the <a href={iphoneAppUrl}>App Store</a>. Then pair it with OpenOrc on your computer:</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>On your computer, open Settings → <strong>Phone</strong> and turn on <strong>Allow paired phones</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Pair a phone</strong>. A pairing code appears, and expires after five minutes.</li>
      <li>On the phone, open OpenOrc, tap <strong>Scan code</strong>, and point the camera at the code.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you can't scan, click <strong>Copy link</strong> under the code on your computer, then tap <strong>Paste</strong> on the phone. With Universal Clipboard, the link reaches the phone without you sending it. To look around before you pair, tap <strong>Try the demo</strong>, which shows the app with a made-up computer.</p>
    <p>Settings → <strong>Phone</strong> lists your paired phones under <strong>Paired phones</strong>, each with when it last connected.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="connection">
    <h2 id="connection">How the phone reaches your computer</h2>
    <p>The phone connects to your computer directly, in one of two ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>On the same network, such as your home or office Wi-Fi.</li>
      <li>Over Tailscale, when both are on your tailnet. This works from anywhere your phone has internet.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each connection is encrypted end to end with keys made on the phone and the computer, so your router, Tailscale, and anyone else on the network can't read or change it. Your computer accepts only the phones paired with it, and only from local and
      Tailscale addresses. The addresses it offers are under <strong>Addresses</strong> in Settings → <strong>Phone</strong>.
    </p>
    <Callout>The phone reaches your computer only while OpenOrc is open there and the computer is awake. When it can't reach your computer, the phone says why, such as no internet or the computer being out of reach.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="use">
    <h2 id="use">What you can do from the phone</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>See your threads as they change, with what each agent is doing and what needs you.</li>
      <li>Open a thread and read the agent's work and reply as they stream in, including its steps, images, and visualizations.</li>
      <li>Send a message, say something during the turn, queue a message, or stop the turn. Answer questions and approvals on their cards.</li>
      <li>Start a thread in any project, with the agent and model you choose and, for Claude, the account. It works where new threads on your computer do, as set by <strong>New thread workspace</strong> in Settings → <a href={docHref("settings") + "#general"}>General</a>, or choose <strong>Cloud</strong> for a sandbox of its own. Attach photos from the camera or your library.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Move to Cloud</strong> in a thread's menu to send it to the cloud, and <strong>Bring to Mac</strong> to bring a thread back from the cloud brain. See <a href={docHref("cloud") + "#move"}>Cloud threads</a>.</li>
      <li>Pin, snooze, rename, mark as read or unread, and resolve threads, from a thread's menu or by swiping its row.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Changes</strong> in a thread's menu, read the diff, commit, push, and open a pull request on GitHub.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Preview</strong>, watch the page the thread's <a href={docHref("browser")}>Preview</a> shows on your computer, and scroll, tap, and type into it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To delete or fork a thread, or move it between the checkout and a worktree, use your computer. The phone's Settings shows your usage, without account details, and lets you pair another computer.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="cloud-brain">
    <h2 id="cloud-brain">Reach the cloud brain</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={docHref("cloud") + "#brain"}>cloud brain</a> is a copy of OpenOrc on your own Vercel or Agent37 account. Pair your phone with it too, and you can follow and start cloud threads while your computer sleeps or is off.
    </p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>On your computer, open Settings → <strong>Cloud</strong>, and under <strong>Cloud brain</strong> click <strong>Pair phone</strong>.</li>
      <li>On the phone, scan the code as you would for your computer.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The phone reaches the brain over the internet, at its address on Vercel or Agent37, encrypted end to end like the link to your computer. Only phones paired with the brain can connect. Opening the app wakes a sleeping brain, on
      Vercel through its door, a small function in your Vercel team. The phone says <strong>Waking</strong> meanwhile.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Home lists the threads on your computer and on the brain together, and marks cloud threads <strong>Cloud</strong>. While your computer sleeps, its threads stay listed from its last answer, dimmed and marked <strong>Mac asleep</strong>.</li>
      <li>A new thread you start in the cloud starts on the brain.</li>
      <li>If the brain holds your Apple push key, it sends notifications too, sealed the same way as your computer's.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Phones paired with the brain are listed under it in Settings → <strong>Cloud</strong>, each with <strong>Remove</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="remove">
    <h2 id="remove">Remove a phone</h2>
    <p>To stop a phone from connecting, click <strong>Remove</strong> beside it in Settings → <strong>Phone</strong>. Your computer deletes its record of the phone, and the key the phone holds stops working. Turning off <strong>Allow paired phones</strong> stops every phone from connecting and keeps their pairing for later.</p>
    <p>On the phone, removing a computer in Settings deletes the phone's key for it and the threads and images it kept. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> lists what the phone keeps and every connection it makes.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "threads" }, { slug: "security" }, { slug: "network" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
